WHO   — THE MOS 6502 — an exereunesis sphere — the teardown
WHAT  — The 6502 is the most consequential cheap chip in computing history. At about a quarter the price of its rivals, this tiny 8-bit processor powered the Apple II, the Commodore 64, the Atari machines, the BBC Micro — and, in derived form, the NES. It put computers and consoles in millions of homes.
WHERE — the teardown — a made machine, opened and explored  Framework inputs: the chips; the buses; the spec; the lore  Conductor / external anchor: ROOT0 (catalogued into UD0)
WHY   — The 6502 is the most consequential cheap chip in computing history. At about a quarter the price of its rivals, this tiny 8-bit processor powered the Apple II, the Commodore 64, the Atari machines, the BBC Micro — and, in derived form, the NES. It put computers and consoles in millions of homes.
